Installing from source requires asciidoc (for a2x), probably DocBook DTD files,
GNU Make, GCC or a compatible compiler (like Clang), and the ar, ld and objcopy
tools (from GNU binutils, LLVM or your system). "make" is usually GNU Make in
desktop Linux distributions, but you may have to call it as "gmake" in other
systems, like the BSDs. The C library must provide getentropy() and
explicit_bzero(), and the build uses ELF shared library conventions. Quick
guide:

make
make test
make install # Usually as root.

To remove the installed files, run "make uninstall" with the same variables
used for "make install".

When running make, you can set values for several variables to modify
compilation, installation and meta-information paths. If you do, you need to
include them in *every* call to make.

Variables:

* CC to set the C compiler. gcc by default, or cc if gcc is not available.
* LDFLAGS for additional linker flags, empty by default.
* AR, ARFLAGS and OBJCOPY to set the archiver, its flags and objcopy. ARFLAGS
  is rcsD by default. If your ar does not support the D (deterministic) flag,
  use ARFLAGS=rcs.
* PREFIX, which is /usr/local by default.
* MANDIR, which is $(PREFIX)/share/man by default.
* INCLUDEDIR, which is $(PREFIX)/include by default.
* LIBDIR, which is $(PREFIX)/lib64 by default if that directory exists, or
  $(PREFIX)/lib otherwise.
* PKGCONFIGDIR, which is $(LIBDIR)/pkgconfig by default, or
  $(PREFIX)/libdata/pkgconfig in FreeBSD.

Only needed in the installation step:

* DESTDIR for a base installation path, empty by default.

When cross-compiling, set CC, AR and OBJCOPY to the tools for the target
system, for example:

make CC=aarch64-linux-gnu-gcc AR=aarch64-linux-gnu-ar OBJCOPY=aarch64-linux-gnu-objcopy

You could also set CFLAGS but, by default, the ones provided by Solar Designer
at crypt_blowfish/Makefile are used. Personally, I recommend to keep them as
they are.
